# Separate correction of delayed selection publication

The prior stage's181 exact artifacts and manifest are preserved byte for byte. Its18 passing focused groups did not cover a delayed read after history restoration. Independent review held that read for entryA, opened entryB, then releasedA's result. The visibleB editor retained its fields while the global selected identity changed toA; savingB wroteB's content intoA. The exact failed source-bound test and requests remain in the independent review and preserved failure copies. New-entry and lock/unlock variants also failed. An ordinary save completion clearing a newer editor was found separately in the pre-history handler and is corrected here within the same asynchronous selection boundary.

The client now invalidates a selection generation before beginning an entry read and checks view, editor, generation and captured identity before publishing its result. History list/preview/restore results additionally check the active history request and row attachment. Post-restore read results remain local until these checks pass. Mutation handlers capture the editor identity before sending a request; their completion cannot clear or render a different editor selected meanwhile. The intended server mutation can finish, but stale UI publication is suppressed. Per-view list generations prevent an older response from replacing a newer search. Session epochs prevent a pre-lock response from rearming the client idle timer after lock.

The original independent wrong-entry test changes only candidate import and owned observation paths. The six boundary tests retain their exact bodies; their fixture adapter defaults to this correction directory and writes observations in owned `.local`. Three additional cases cover out-of-order OpenA/OpenB reads, closing a held historical preview and an older search result while a newer result/draft is visible. The overlapping-search case deliberately re-enables the modeled button to exercise two requests; it does not claim that normal browser users can bypass the disabled button.

Server, history algorithm, KDBX and XML implementation are unchanged from the old stage. New evidence reruns the original18 groups plus10 selection groups against the corrected client and copied host. These are finite DOM element-model tests, not browser or original GUI executions. Original24 assembly-helper executions and source-assisted edit scopes remain separately counted. No production integration, complete product parity, original-grade security or universal superiority is claimed.

Use `record.mjs selection-fixed-final history.test.mjs unknown-time.test.mjs http.test.mjs ui.test.mjs selection-independent.test.mjs selection-boundaries.test.mjs selection-adjacent.test.mjs` in this stage for a new source-bound run. The copied old finalize/run receipts remain historical; `selection-finalize.mjs` creates the correction's own receipt and strict manifest. Do not run old finalizers to replace historical evidence.
